{
    "title": "Letter From the President to United States Steel Corporation Senior Vice President, General Counsel and Secretary Scot Duncan",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 25, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-25/pdf/2025-21204.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has activated special oversight powers over U.S. Steel, saying the company\u2019s American operations are essential to national security. The President named two Commerce Department officials to monitor the company and take a board seat to help ensure U.S.-based plants keep running.\n\nIn a formal letter to U.S. Steel, the President said he holds a special class of stock\u2014often called a \u201cgolden share\u201d\u2014under a security agreement involving the U.S. government, Nippon Steel, and U.S. Steel. Using those rights, he designated Under Secretary William Kimmitt to exercise certain oversight authorities and appointed David Shapiro as the government\u2019s director on U.S. Steel\u2019s board. These appointments remain in place while both serve in government, unless changed.\n\nWho stands to benefit? American steelworkers and communities around U.S. Steel plants may see added stability if the move helps keep facilities operating. U.S. manufacturers that depend on domestic steel, as well as defense and infrastructure projects, could gain from a clearer commitment to production at home. Investors who value continuity and government support for strategic industry may also view this as a stabilizing step.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? U.S. Steel\u2019s management and some shareholders could face tighter constraints, slower decision-making, or added compliance costs under closer government oversight. International partners, including Nippon Steel, may encounter limits that complicate integration or strategy. Some market participants may worry about greater political influence over corporate governance, potential legal friction, or a chilling effect on future cross-border investment.\n\nOverall, this move signals that maintaining domestic steel production is a priority, while raising fresh questions about corporate independence, global partnerships, and what this means for long-term investment decisions in the sector.",
